Vitreojack

Ingredients: AMBIENT + NEW JACK SWING / SWINGBEAT + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Choir  
Creative Catalyst: The style was codified by grandmothers who DJ at a floating market before dawn.

Vitreojack is a hallucinatory collision of humid morning drones, aggressively swung 1990s swingbeat percussion, and weightless matriarchal choral arrangements. It sounds like an R&B street-party cassette dropped into a canal at 4:00 AM: slow-dissolving vocal harmonies float like morning mist over river water, suddenly intercepted by the stiff, razor-sharp shuffle of quantized MPC drum machines and pitch-bent, tea-house psychedelia.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 98–106 BPM. The pulse is built on an extreme swing ratio (64–72%), transplanting Teddy Riley's crisp, skittering New Jack Swing hi-hats and gated snares into a sluggish, waterlogged tempo that sways like a moored wooden sampan.
- Harmonic Palette: Pentatonic morning ragas melted into lush R&B minor-ninth and suspended-fourth chords; microtonal choir clusters that glide downward via tape-drag rather than standard western resolution.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Annealed Master Bus: Submitting drum tracks through a hot-blown glass resonator—a custom acoustic chamber made from hand-blown glass globes that saturate high-end frequencies with fragile, brittle overtone rings.
  2. Canal-Water Phasing: Running vocal choir sends through submerged hydrophones positioned alongside idling two-stroke long-tail boat motors to produce natural comb filtering and deep-water phasing.
  3. Zero-Grid Gated Mist: Gating sprawling 40-second ambient synthesizer decays with the sharp, aggressive sidechain triggers of classic swingbeat ghost snares.
- Dynamics: Songs open in near-complete ambient stillness—only water ripples and drifting multi-part choir humming—before a sudden, dry, bone-snapping swingbeat kick-snare pattern locks in, alternating between weightless vapor and physical, hip-winding bounce.

Scene & Origin Lore

Codified along the labyrinthine canals of the Taling Chan and Amphawa floating markets, Vitreojack emerged when a collective of septuagenarian fruit vendors and temple choir singers—known locally as the Mae Krua Mixers—began strapping vintage 12-bit samplers and PA horns to their produce barges. Dressing in silk floral aprons, wide woven palm hats (ngob), and heavy amber pendants, these grandmothers took over the dawn hours (between 3:30 AM and sunrise) before tourism choked the canals. They layered field recordings of boiling coconut sugar and river current beneath loops of forgotten 1991 swingbeat cassettes, singing ancient Buddhist blessing chants in microtonal, four-part harmony over the top. The market transformed from a trade hub into a floating psychedelic sound clash where vendors traded jackfruit for fresh sample diskettes, their skiffs gently bumping gunwales in sync with the drunken swing of the drum machines.

Typical Instruments
- Rhythm & Groove: E-mu SP-1200, Akai MPC60, Roland TR-808 (with long-decay sub-bass pitched down), wooden market pestles and metal ladles sampled into 12-bit grain.
- Synthesizers & Atmosphere: Roland D-50, Yamaha DX7 (playing glass-chime and watery electric piano patches), tape-saturated Mellotron choirs, Korg Wavestation vector pads.
- Choral & Acoustic: Live six-woman matriarchal vocal ensemble, amplified Thai phin (lute) run through reverse tape delays, temple gongs dipped in water troughs while struck.

For Fans Of: Teddy Riley producing Harold Budd, Julianna Barwick remixed by Jimmy Jam & Terry Lewis, Oneohtrix Point Never, late-era Cocteau Twins.

